WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.913
Pleased to meet you! My name is Albert Dunbar and I am a 22-year-old actor living and working in London, England, in 1612.

2
00:00:11.914 --> 00:00:20.188
Some women who come to see me on stage say that I am the best-looking man in London, and I tend to agree. A

3
00:00:20.189 --> 00:00:30.409
good actor must pay attention to his appearance at all times, as well as to his speech and body movement, in order to get the best roles. Of

4
00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:40.050
course I only appear in the most popular productions, especially the plays written by William Shakespeare, the greatest writer in all of England. My

5
00:00:40.051 --> 00:00:47.193
career began when I was only seven years old and the church organist in my little village heard me sing. He

6
00:00:47.194 --> 00:00:54.747
immediately told my father that I should audition for the children’s choir at the Chapel Royal to sing for Queen Elizabeth. Excited,

7
00:00:54.748 --> 00:01:03.983
my father took me to St. James’ Palace to audition, and afterward I was invited to be a member of the choir. It

8
00:01:03.984 --> 00:01:12.656
wasn’t long before I was acting in plays for the queen too, since boys with high voices are needed to play all the female roles. No

9
00:01:12.657 --> 00:01:16.900
women are allowed on stage; it just isn’t done. I

10
00:01:16.901 --> 00:01:20.151
had lots of fun wearing wigs and playing different parts, but

11
00:01:20.152 --> 00:01:26.631
at times there were tense moments when some of the plays poked fun at the people in the queen’s court. The

12
00:01:26.632 --> 00:01:32.806
nobility—the lords and ladies—became very annoyed at the writers and actors. One

13
00:01:32.807 --> 00:01:41.506
time Queen Elizabeth became so angry that everyone in the show was briefly put in jail for offending her. When

14
00:01:41.507 --> 00:01:50.699
I was 13, Queen Elizabeth died, and I soon joined an acting company associated with the Globe Theater called the King’s Men. The

15
00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:58.580
company members said they liked my pretty looks and my sweet-sounding voice and hired me on as an apprentice. But most

16
00:01:58.581 --> 00:02:06.358
of all, they liked my ability to read since many young boys in England cannot read or write. There were

17
00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:10.923
other boys in the company, ranging in age from 10 to 15, and the

18
00:02:10.924 --> 00:02:17.728
older actors taught us how to speak loudly so any large audience would be able to hear us. We learned

19
00:02:17.729 --> 00:02:26.452
the galliard, a dance with quick steps and high jumps, and the canary, a very popular dance from Spain. A music

20
00:02:26.453 --> 00:02:31.880
teacher taught us how to play the fiddle, lute, and recorder. Dancing and

21
00:02:31.881 --> 00:02:41.188
musicianship are both skills we need for our various roles since the audience always enjoys a lively song! But I

22
00:02:41.189 --> 00:02:49.179
must tell you, whenever I sing the sad song "Barbara Allen," the theater flows with tears. All the

23
00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:58.016
apprentices also learned how to fight with wooden swords for the fight scenes, which are the most exciting moments in the theater. I have

24
00:02:58.017 --> 00:03:02.531
come a long way from the choir, I can tell you! As a

25
00:03:02.532 --> 00:03:14.542
boy player, I was given spending money, which I used to buy treats such as oranges and gingerbread. I also bought books—I love poetry. However, we

26
00:03:14.543 --> 00:03:22.005
were fined if we were late, missed a rehearsal, or damaged our costumes. Costumes are

27
00:03:22.006 --> 00:03:30.709
the biggest expense for a theater company since they are basically the clothes that people wear on the very best of occasions. Staining a

28
00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:36.744
richly embroidered doublet or tearing a velvet cape is extremely serious; the Globe

29
00:03:36.745 --> 00:03:41.491
acting company spends hundreds of pounds yearly on costumes. Since

30
00:03:41.492 --> 00:03:47.995
we use very little scenery, elaborate costumes are really important. When

31
00:03:47.996 --> 00:03:55.382
a flag flies from the roof of the theater, people know that there will be a performance that afternoon. Now

32
00:03:55.383 --> 00:04:02.074
that I am a grown man and famous, an apprentice helps me dress backstage. Of

33
00:04:02.075 --> 00:04:11.640
course, since my voice changed, I no longer play women’s parts; now I play the lead male roles, and I’m excellent at them. But

34
00:04:11.641 --> 00:04:14.186
even though I am a celebrity, I

35
00:04:14.187 --> 00:04:21.623
still can’t help peeking at the audience through the curtain like a novice to see how many people have come to see our play. The

36
00:04:21.624 --> 00:04:30.650
groundlings are the poorer folk who must stand on the ground right in front of the stage. They pay a penny to get in. The

37
00:04:30.651 --> 00:04:35.317
wealthy pay more and have seats on the upper balconies of the theater. Vendors

38
00:04:35.318 --> 00:04:41.694
sell fruit and nuts, and young boys run back and forth delivering bottles of ale. People

39
00:04:41.695 --> 00:04:46.995
converse and some play card games while they wait for the show to begin. It

40
00:04:46.996 --> 00:04:58.589
is very noisy, but when the trumpet blasts three times, people know the play is about to start and they quiet down expectantly. Today

41
00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:09.800
we are performing Macbeth , a play with a Scottish king, a ghost, three witches, soldiers, and lots of blood.

42
00:05:09.801 --> 00:05:14.980
People are quite fond of it, and most actors play several parts.

43
00:05:14.981 --> 00:05:19.709
Of course I am playing the leading role of Macbeth.

44
00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:29.368
So when I arrived at the theater this morning, I paced backstage practicing my lines to make sure I had memorized them all.

45
00:05:29.369 --> 00:05:38.280
There is a man, called a book holder, who holds a copy of the play near the stage so he can whisper a line if an actor forgets.

46
00:05:38.281 --> 00:05:44.979
But I like to be totally prepared, and I’ve never needed any prompting.

47
00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:51.481
This morning the stage manager had a difficult time getting the throne in place for the actor who plays the king.

48
00:05:51.482 --> 00:05:58.082
He also had trouble making the trapdoor work perfectly so the ghost can disappear in act 2.

49
00:05:58.083 --> 00:06:06.305
Some apprentices bustled about before the play, filling pigs' bladders with blood so the sword-fighting scenes would look real.

50
00:06:06.306 --> 00:06:13.752
The actors hide the bladders under their arms and then break them open when another actor pretends to stab them.

51
00:06:13.753 --> 00:06:18.687
It looks very gory when the blood spills all over the place.

52
00:06:18.688 --> 00:06:24.693
It’s a great trick, and audiences love it!

53
00:06:24.694 --> 00:06:33.109
Actors frequently perform on other stages around London, such as the Swan Theater, the Middle Temple Theater, and the Boar’s Head Inn,

54
00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:36.611
which the owner renovated so plays could be put on there.

55
00:06:36.612 --> 00:06:43.650
We also travel all over the countryside to do shows in town squares and at local inns.

56
00:06:43.651 --> 00:06:50.320
Last year I was invited to the private estate of an earl to provide the entertainment after supper.

57
00:06:50.321 --> 00:06:52.786
But by the time I made my entrance,

58
00:06:52.787 --> 00:07:02.528
a few of the guests were fast asleep and snoring so loudly that no one could hear me sing my famous version of "Barbara Allen."

59
00:07:02.529 --> 00:07:11.678
I was paid well for my appearance, but I far prefer being at the Globe where my artistic nature is taken more seriously.

60
00:07:11.679 --> 00:07:13.833
Come see me when you are in London.

61
00:07:13.834 --> 00:07:21.331
Everyone knows me. Just ask for the great Albert Dunbar!

